  "final_text": "The dashboard and the rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't checked that the rule ran cleanly. Its first execution status was still \"pending\" when I created it. I also didn't open the dashboard in Kibana to see how the panels render.\n\n**What the data holds:** The Kibana Sample Data Logs data view is web-server traffic.\n- **Request details:** `timestamp`, `clientip`, `request`, `url`, `extension`, `referer`, `agent`, `host`, `message`.\n- **Response:** `response` (the HTTP code, a text field with a `.keyword` subfield I used for filtering), `bytes`.\n- **Geo and machine:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`, `machine.os`, `machine.ram`.\n- **Other:** `memory`, `phpmemory`, `tags`, `hour_of_day`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-1685382b Web logs overview\" (id `38f9cdce-724f-4f76-9f26-40b7a34cbbb7`) defaults to the last 7 days and has five panels:\n- Three metric panels: total requests, average bytes and unique clients.\n- A line chart of requests over time.\n- A bar chart of requests by response code.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-1685382b 5xx spike\" (id `a11b2057-c49a-420d-bdda-3a08312c92b9`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, counts documents whose `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]` over a 5-minute window, and fires when the count is above 10.\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, because the instance has no connectors. It will show alerts in Kibana but won't send notifications anywhere.",
